{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789497234,"nonce":"qK7j5Hwu/dxgYI15PIM1MFhysAY=","title":"b4: search failures are indistinguishable from empty search results","message":"Problem\n-------\n\n``get_pi_search_results()`` returns ``None`` for two different outcomes: the\nserver failed, and the server answered with nothing. Callers cannot tell them\napart, and every caller treats ``None`` as nothing-found. A failed search\ntherefore reads as \"there is nothing to find\", which is a wrong answer rather\nthan a visible failure.\n\nCode path\n---------\n\n``get_pi_search_results()`` -- ``src/b4/__init__.py:4342-4364``:\n\n- ``4357-4359``: ``except liblore.RemoteError`` logs ``Server returned an\n  error.`` at info level and returns ``None``.\n- ``4360-4362``: an empty mbox logs ``No messages found for that query`` and\n  returns ``None``.\n\nCallers, all of which collapse the two:\n\n- ``ez.py:1913-1915`` -- ``b4 trailers`` change-id lookup:\n  ``if smsgs is not None: list_msgs += smsgs``. A failed search contributes\n  nothing and the command proceeds to report no new trailers.\n- ``mbox.py:404``, ``mbox.py:780``, ``dig.py:166``, ``dig.py:186``,\n  ``dig.py:246``, ``__init__.py:423``, ``__init__.py:4391``,\n  ``__init__.py:4426``, ``__init__.py:5582``, ``__init__.py:5793``.\n\nThe log line does distinguish the cases, but it is ``logger.info`` amid\nordinary progress output, and nothing in the return contract carries it.\n\nImpact\n------\n\nWorst for trailer collection, where the answer is used to decide whether a\nseries has picked up new tags. A search that failed is reported as a series\nwith no new trailers, so a maintainer can apply a series while silently\nmissing Reviewed-by/Tested-by trailers that were posted. Nothing warns that\nthe lookup did not actually happen.\n\nRelated\n-------\n\nliblore ``77478f5`` (``node: search 4xx from a fallback origin is not retried\non canonical``) is the upstream half: a fallback origin without search\nindexes can make a search fail even though the canonical origin could have\nanswered it. That bug produces the failure; this bug is why b4 presents the\nfailure as an empty result. The two are independent -- fixing either one\nalone still leaves a wrong answer reachable, because liblore cannot rule out\na genuinely failing search and b4 would still swallow it.\n\nExposure grows with the v0.17 plan to replace ``b4.midmask`` with a\nmulti-valued ``b4.piurl`` and let projects ship mirror lists in\n``.b4-config``: fallback origins reach users who did not choose them.\n\nProven vs. inferred\n-------------------\n\nProven by reading code: both outcomes return ``None`` at ``4359`` and\n``4362``; every listed caller treats ``None`` as nothing-found; the\ndistinguishing message is info-level only.\n\nNot verified at runtime: no command was executed for this report, so the\nmaintainer-visible symptom (a series applied without its posted trailers) is\npredicted from the call path rather than observed.","files":null}]}